

A Traffic-Aware Benchmark Study of
Quantum-Inspired Particle Swarm Optimization for
Large-Scale Vehicle Routing
Shlok Goenka
Computer Applications
Manipal University Jaipur
shlokgoenka2508@gmail.com
Abstract
Vehicle routing in real transportation systems is complicated not only by combinatorial route
selection, but also by time-dependent travel conditions, vehicle capacity constraints, and customer
time windows.  This paper presents a research-oriented simulation and benchmarking framework for a
traffic-aware Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem with Time Windows (CVRPTW). The framework
represents transportation networks as directed weighted graphs and evaluates routes using sequential,
departure-time dependent travel times.  A deterministic sinusoidal traffic model is used to represent
time-varying congestion on road links.
The principal optimization method investigated is Quantum-Inspired Particle Swarm Optimization
(QPSO), implemented as a classically executable population-based metaheuristic using random-key
encoding to map continuous search vectors to discrete vehicle routes.  QPSO is evaluated against four
baselines:  conventional Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO), a Genetic Algorithm (GA), uniform
Random Search, and Adaptive Large Neighborhood Search (ALNS). All methods use a common route
evaluator and identical traffic scenarios, enabling paired comparisons under static and dynamic traffic
conditions.
The experimental design spans synthetic transportation networks containing 20, 50, 100, 200,
300, 400, and 500 nodes, with repeated instances and independent stochastic runs.  Performance
is assessed using objective value, runtime, objective-evaluation count, convergence, feasibility, and
constraint violations.  At the time of writing, the large-scale benchmark is being executed; consequently,
numerical performance claims are intentionally left as placeholders in this manuscript and must be
populated only after the benchmark is complete.  The study is designed to determine whether QPSO
provides a meaningful computational advantage under time-dependent traffic, rather than assuming
such an advantage a priori.
Keywords:  vehicle routing problem; dynamic traffic; time-dependent routing; quantum-inspired op-
timization; QPSO; particle swarm optimization; adaptive large neighborhood search; metaheuristics;
CVRPTW
1    Introduction
Vehicle routing is a fundamental combinatorial optimization problem in transportation, logistics, delivery,
dispatch, and fleet management.  The classical Vehicle Routing Problem (VRP) seeks a set of vehicle
routes that serves geographically distributed customers while minimizing a specified routing cost subject
to operational constraints.  The problem was introduced in its modern form by Dantzig and Ramser in
the context of gasoline delivery, where vehicle capacity and customer service requirements were already
central to the formulation [1].
Real transportation networks,  however,  are rarely static.  Travel times can change throughout a
planning horizon because of congestion, incidents, demand fluctuations, signal timing, weather, and other
effects.  This motivates Dynamic Vehicle Routing Problems (DVRPs) and time-dependent routing models.
Reviews of the field emphasize that dynamic information and time-dependent travel times fundamentally
change both the modelling and algorithmic requirements of routing [9, 10, 11].
The computational difficulty of large-scale VRPs makes exact optimization impractical for many real-
istic instances.  Consequently, heuristic and metaheuristic methods play a central role in practical routing
research.  Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO), Genetic Algorithms (GA), Large Neighborhood Search, and
1

Adaptive Large Neighborhood Search (ALNS) are examples of population-based or neighborhood-based
methods that can explore large combinatorial search spaces without requiring an exact enumeration of
all feasible routes.  ALNS, in particular, has demonstrated strong performance on constrained routing
problems through adaptive selection of multiple destroy and repair operators [7, 8].
Quantum-Inspired Particle Swarm Optimization (QPSO) is a classical metaheuristic motivated by
quantum-behaved particle dynamics.  It modifies the conventional velocity-based PSO mechanism through
a  probabilistic  position-update  rule  involving  the  mean  best  position  and  a  contraction–expansion
coefficient [4,5].  Importantly,  QPSO does  not  require a quantum computer:  the algorithm used in
this study is entirely classically executable.  The term “quantum-inspired” refers to the mathematical
construction of the search process rather than to physical quantum computation.
This work investigates QPSO in a traffic-aware routing setting.  Rather than assuming that QPSO
is superior to classical optimization, the study asks whether its search behaviour remains competitive
when the travel cost of an edge depends on the time at which that edge is traversed.  The comparison
is made against four baselines:  standard PSO, GA, uniform Random Search, and ALNS. The use of a
common objective evaluator and paired traffic scenarios is intended to isolate algorithmic differences from
differences in problem evaluation.
The main contributions of this work are:
1.  a modular formulation of a time-dependent CVRPTW on a directed transportation graph;
2.
a reproducible edge-level dynamic traffic model in which congestion and travel time vary with departure
time;
3.  a QPSO implementation using random-key encoding for discrete vehicle routing;
4.
a common objective evaluator shared by QPSO and all classical baselines, reducing evaluation bias
between algorithms;
5.  an ALNS baseline based on adaptive destroy and repair operations;
6.
a paired experimental design comparing five optimization methods under both static and dynamic
traffic across network sizes from 20 to 500 nodes; and
7.an empirical framework for identifying the strengths and failure modes of QPSO before designing any
future hybrid or improved quantum-inspired method.
The paper deliberately separates the methodological contribution from the empirical conclusion.  In
particular, no claim that QPSO outperforms classical methods is made before the benchmark results are
available.
2    Background and Related Work
2.1    Vehicle Routing
The VRP generalizes the Traveling Salesman Problem to a fleet of vehicles serving multiple customers.  A
typical capacitated formulation assigns customers to vehicle routes while ensuring that every customer is
visited and that the total demand assigned to each vehicle does not exceed its capacity [1, 2].
The Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem with Time Windows (CVRPTW) adds service-time and
temporal restrictions.  Each customerihas a time window [a
i
,b
i
], and service outside that interval may
either be forbidden or, as in the experimental framework of this study, represented through an explicit
penalty.
2.2    Dynamic and Time-Dependent Routing
Time-dependent routing considers networks in which the traversal time of an edge varies with the departure
time.  Gendreau et al. describe time-dependent routing as the design of best routes in a graph whose arc
traversal times vary over the planning horizon [10].  More recent work has expanded this area to include
travel-time prediction, real-time reoptimization, dynamic discretization, and machine-learning-assisted
approaches [11].
Dynamic VRP research distinguishes between different sources and degrees of dynamism, including
dynamically changing travel conditions and dynamically revealed customer requests [9,12].  The present
2

study focuses on a narrower deterministic setting:  all customers are known in advance, while travel
conditions vary continuously as a function of departure time.
2.3    Particle Swarm Optimization
Particle Swarm Optimization was introduced by Kennedy and Eberhart as a population-based stochastic
optimization method inspired by collective behaviour [3].  Standard PSO updates each particle using its
current velocity, its own historically best position, and the best position found by the swarm.
In discrete routing applications, a continuous PSO representation must be mapped to permutations or
routes.  The present implementation uses random-key encoding so that PSO and QPSO can operate in
the same continuous search space and use the same route decoder.
2.4    Quantum-Inspired Particle Swarm Optimization
QPSO modifies the conventional PSO dynamics using a probabilistic quantum-behaved position update.
Sun, Feng, and Xu introduced a formulation in which particles are attracted toward local attractors
while the contraction–expansion coefficient controls the search scale [4].  Subsequent work developed
global-search and parameter-control strategies for QPSO [5] and analysed individual particle behaviour
and convergence properties [6].
The term quantum-inspired must be interpreted carefully in this work.  No qubits, quantum gates,
quantum annealing hardware, or quantum speedup are used.  QPSO is evaluated as a classical stochastic
optimization algorithm.
2.5    Adaptive Large Neighborhood Search
ALNS extends Large Neighborhood Search by maintaining multiple destroy and repair operators and
adapting their selection according to their observed performance.  Ropke and Pisinger demonstrated
the effectiveness of this framework on constrained pickup-and-delivery routing problems, and later work
established ALNS as a general-purpose routing metaheuristic [7, 8].
ALNS is included in this study as a strong route-oriented classical baseline.  This is important because
comparing QPSO only against simple random search or a weak evolutionary baseline could create an
artificial impression of algorithmic superiority.
