and mutation is applied with probability p
m
.
The resulting offspring are decoded into vehicle routes and evaluated using the same objective and
constraint-handling procedure used by the other population-based algorithms.
4.5    Random Search
Random Search provides a simple stochastic baseline.  At each iteration, candidate random-key vectors
are independently sampled according to
x
i
∼ U (0, 1),   i = 1,...,N.(27)
Each candidate is decoded and evaluated, and the best solution found within the allocated evaluation
budget is retained.
4.6    Adaptive Large Neighborhood Search
ALNS provides a route-space optimization baseline that does not use the random-key representation.
At each iteration, a portion of the current solution is destroyed using a selected removal operator
and subsequently reconstructed using an insertion operator.  The algorithm employs multiple destroy
and repair operators whose selection probabilities are adaptively updated according to their observed
performance.
Candidate solutions may be accepted according to a simulated-annealing acceptance criterion, allowing
the search to escape local optima.
The destruction fraction is selected within the interval
ρ∈ [ρ
min
,ρ
max
],(28)
where ρ
min
= 0.1 and ρ
max
= 0.3 in the experimental configuration.
5    Fairness of the Comparison
A central design principle of the benchmark is that the algorithms should differ primarily in their search
strategy rather than in the problem they are asked to solve.
For each problem instance, the following are held constant across methods:
1.  transportation graph;
2.  depot;
3.  customer locations;
4.  customer demands;
5.  service times;
6.  time windows;
7.  fleet size and vehicle capacity;
8.  traffic scenario;
9.  objective function;
10.  penalty coefficients; and
11.  maximum objective-evaluation budget.
7

The  benchmark  uses  paired  static  and  dynamic  traffic  conditions.   Thus,  for  a  given  instance,
QPSO/static, PSO/static, GA/static, Random/static, and ALNS/static are evaluated on the same static
environment, while the corresponding dynamic runs use the same dynamic traffic scenario.
This pairing is important because independently generated traffic conditions could otherwise introduce
environmental variance that would be mistaken for algorithmic variance.
6    Experimental Design
6.1    Network Scale
The benchmark covers seven network sizes:
N ∈{20, 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500}.(29)
The purpose of increasing the network size is not merely to report a single best objective value.  It is
to investigate how solution quality, convergence, feasibility, and computational cost scale with problem
size.
6.2    Instance Generation
For each network size, 10 independently generated problem instances are used.  Each instance contains a
central depot and customer nodes embedded in the synthetic transportation graph.
The benchmark therefore contains
7× 10 = 70(30)
base instances.
6.3    Repeated Runs
Each algorithm is executed for five stochastic runs per instance and traffic condition.
With five algorithms and two traffic conditions, the planned number of optimizer executions is
7× 10× 5× 5× 2 = 3500.(31)
The benchmark uses independently generated stochastic seeds.  The exact seed used by each run
should be retained in the experiment metadata so that the final reported results remain reproducible.
6.4    Evaluation Budget
Each run is limited to
E
max
= 5000(32)
objective evaluations, with a maximum iteration/generation limit of 1000.
Using an objective-evaluation budget rather than comparing only iteration counts is important because
one iteration of ALNS can evaluate a different number of candidate solutions than one iteration of QPSO
or PSO.
If every run consumes the complete budget, the benchmark corresponds to a maximum of
3500× 5000 = 17.5× 10
6
(33)
objective evaluations.
