8    Implementation Architecture
The simulator is implemented as a modular Python research framework.  The main components are:
1.  a transportation-graph layer;
2.  a VRP instance representation;
3.  immutable static and dynamic traffic models;
4.  a shared objective evaluator;
5.  optimizer-specific implementations;
6.  a benchmark runner;
7.  result aggregation; and
8.  a Matplotlib-based visualization layer.
The separation between traffic generation and objective evaluation is important.  The optimizer does
not directly manipulate the traffic state.  Instead, it proposes a route and the evaluator queries the traffic
model at the departure time of each edge.
The result system stores raw run-level data before aggregation.  This permits later reanalysis without
rerunning the optimization process.
9    Experimental Hypotheses
The study is exploratory rather than confirmation-driven.  Nevertheless, several hypotheses motivate the
benchmark.
9.1    H1:  QPSO versus Standard PSO
Because QPSO uses a different stochastic search mechanism and does not rely on velocity accumulation,
it may exhibit different exploration–exploitation behaviour from standard PSO when the route landscape
becomes increasingly discontinuous after random-key decoding.
10

9.2    H2:  QPSO versus GA
The  comparison  with  GA  tests  whether  the  QPSO  search  mechanism  provides  an  advantage  over
evolutionary variation operators under the same random-key representation.
9.3    H3:  QPSO versus Random Search
Random Search establishes a weak but useful lower-bound baseline for algorithmic sophistication.  QPSO
should outperform uniform sampling if its population-guided search mechanism is providing meaningful
optimization value.
9.4    H4:  QPSO versus ALNS
ALNS provides the most important route-oriented classical comparison in the current study.  If QPSO
is competitive with ALNS under the same evaluation budget, this would provide substantially stronger
evidence than comparisons against random search or simple population methods alone.
Conversely, if ALNS consistently dominates QPSO, that result is also valuable:  it would identify a
limitation of the current QPSO representation or search mechanism and motivate subsequent algorithmic
improvements.
9.5    H5:  Effect of Dynamic Traffic
The relative ranking of algorithms may change when travel times become time-dependent.  An optimizer
that performs well under static costs may not retain the same advantage when the objective landscape
changes as a vehicle moves through the network.
