10.4    Dynamic-Traffic Effect
The difference between static and dynamic performance should be examined for each algorithm.  The
analysis should focus on whether traffic dynamics change:
•  the final objective;
•  feasibility;
•  convergence speed;
•  runtime; and
•  relative algorithm ranking.
10.5    Scaling Behaviour
The final analysis should plot objective quality and runtime against network size.  Particular attention
should be paid to the transition from small instances, where many methods may find similar solutions, to
larger instances where the representation and search mechanism become more important.
11    Discussion
11.1    Interpreting QPSO Performance
The central purpose of the experiment is not to establish that a method labelled “quantum-inspired”
is automatically superior.  Any advantage must be demonstrated empirically against strong classical
baselines.
If QPSO outperforms ALNS under a subset of network sizes or traffic conditions, the result should be
analysed in terms of the observed search behaviour rather than attributed vaguely to “quantum effects.”
Possible interpretations include differences in exploration, population diversity, or the interaction between
QPSO’s continuous dynamics and random-key route encoding.
If ALNS outperforms QPSO consistently, that would suggest that direct route-space neighborhood
search is better matched to the current CVRPTW representation.  Such a result would be scientifically
useful because it would provide a concrete failure mode from which a future QPSO improvement could
be designed.
11.2    Dynamic Traffic
Dynamic traffic introduces a moving evaluation landscape in the sense that the cost of a route depends on
the sequence and timing of its traversals.  The same customer permutation can therefore incur different
travel times depending on when each edge is reached.
An algorithm that simply produces good static routes may not necessarily perform well under this
condition.  The static-versus-dynamic comparison therefore tests robustness to temporal variation rather
than only static solution quality.
11.3    Importance of the Common Evaluator
Because all algorithms are evaluated through the same objective evaluator, differences in objective values
are more directly attributable to their search procedures.  This does not eliminate all sources of bias.
ALNS, QPSO, PSO, GA, and Random Search have inherently different internal operations, and equal
objective-evaluation budgets do not imply equal wall-clock costs.
Nevertheless, equal evaluation budgets provide a defensible common measure of optimization effort.
12    Limitations
Several limitations should be considered when interpreting the results.
13

12.1    Synthetic Networks
The benchmark uses synthetically generated road networks rather than real-world road-network data.
Consequently, the results should not be interpreted as direct evidence of production fleet-routing perfor-
mance.
12.2    Single Depot
The current model contains one central depot.  Multi-depot VRP variants are not addressed.
12.3    Deterministic Traffic Model
The dynamic traffic process is deterministic once its edge parameters and phase values are fixed.  It
represents temporal variation but does not model stochastic incidents, accidents, weather, or probabilistic
traffic forecasts.
12.4    Internal Path Selection
For sparse networks, the evaluator may connect two route waypoints through a shortest physical-distance
path when no direct edge exists.  Traffic is then evaluated on the edges of that path using their departure
times.   The  internal  path  itself  is  not  reoptimized  as  a  function  of  the  departure  time.   Thus,  the
current framework is time-dependent at the edge-evaluation level but does not yet implement a fully
time-dependent shortest-path oracle.
12.5    Random-Key Decoder
The greedy capacity-splitting decoder is simple and reproducible, but it can restrict the set of route
structures accessible to QPSO, PSO, GA, and Random Search.  Performance should therefore be attributed
to the complete optimizer–representation–decoder combination rather than to QPSO alone.
12.6    Soft Constraints
Time-window and structural constraints are represented through penalties in the shared evaluator.  This
permits  optimization  to  proceed  through  infeasible  regions  but  also  means  that  penalty  coefficients
influence the search landscape.
12.7    ALNS Implementation
The ALNS implementation used here is a research baseline, not a claim to represent every state-of-the-art
ALNS design.  Operator selection, repair strategies, cooling parameters, and neighborhood definitions can
substantially influence ALNS performance.
12.8    No Real Quantum Computation
The QPSO algorithm is quantum-inspired but classically executed.  No claim of quantum computational
speedup is made.
13    Future Work
The benchmark is intended to provide evidence for the next stage of the research rather than being the
final algorithmic endpoint.
13.1    Failure-Mode-Driven QPSO Improvement
After the benchmark is complete, QPSO’s behaviour should be analysed to identify specific failure modes.
Examples include:
•  premature convergence;
•  insufficient local refinement;
14

•  poor route-space exploration;
•  sensitivity to the random-key decoder;
•  deterioration at large network sizes; or
•  poor adaptation to dynamic traffic.
Any proposed improvement should target an observed failure rather than adding an arbitrary collection
of heuristics.
13.2    Hybrid Search
A future hybrid method could combine the global exploration behaviour of QPSO with a route-level
local-search or ALNS refinement stage.  Such a method would need an ablation study to determine
whether each added component actually contributes to performance.
A possible architecture is
QPSO exploration→ route decoding→ local/ALNS refinement→ evaluation.(41)
However, this is a future hypothesis, not a result of the present study.
13.3    QUBO and Ising Formulations
Another future direction is a binary formulation of the routing problem.  Binary decision variables can
encode route assignment and sequencing, after which constraints can be incorporated into a Quadratic
Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) objective using penalty terms.
A binary variable x
r
can be transformed into an Ising spin variable s
r
∈{−1, +1} through
x
r
=
1 + s
r
2
.(42)
This transformation is algebraic and does not itself provide quantum computational power.  Its main
value would be to create a formulation that could later be investigated with quantum annealing or
gate-model methods.
A full CVRPTW-to-QUBO transformation is nontrivial because capacity, sequencing, subtour elimi-
nation, and time-window constraints can produce large numbers of binary variables and penalty terms.
Therefore, a future QUBO study should first establish a compact static formulation before attempting a
full dynamic formulation.
14    Conclusion
This  paper  presented  a  traffic-aware  benchmark  framework  for  studying  Quantum-Inspired  Particle
Swarm Optimization in a large-scale vehicle routing setting.  The problem is formulated as a single-depot
CVRPTW  on  a  directed  synthetic  transportation  graph,  with  travel  time  determined  by  edge-level
congestion and, in the dynamic condition, by the time at which each edge is traversed.
The proposed experimental framework compares QPSO with standard PSO, GA, Random Search,
and ALNS using a common evaluator and paired traffic scenarios.  The benchmark spans network sizes
from 20 to 500 nodes and records objective quality, runtime, objective evaluations, convergence, feasibility,
and constraint violations.
The principal methodological objective is to determine the actual operating regime of QPSO rather
than presuppose its superiority.  The resulting benchmark will establish whether QPSO is competitive
with route-oriented classical metaheuristics, whether its behaviour changes under dynamic traffic, and
how its performance scales with network size.
Once these observations are available,  they can be used to formulate a targeted next-generation
quantum-inspired routing method.  In particular, failure modes revealed by the benchmark can motivate
hybrid QPSO–local search strategies or alternative representations, while a separate QUBO and Ising
formulation can provide a bridge toward future quantum optimization research.
15

Data and Code Availability
The simulator and experiment framework are maintained in the project repository:
https://github.com/Sguy1908/Quantum-inspired-intelligent-traffic-route-optimization
The final manuscript should additionally provide the exact benchmark configuration, seeds or seed
manifest, raw result files, and generated figures used to obtain the reported numerical results.
